One common source of vulnerabilities in Java web applications is the improper handling of user input when interacting with the operating system, which can lead to Command Injection.

```java
import java.io.IOException;

public class CommandInjectionExample {
    // This method is vulnerable to command injection if 'userInput' contains shell metacharacters
    public void processImage(String userInput) throws IOException {
        // The runtime.exec method is used incorrectly by concatenating unsanitized input 
        // into a shell command, allowing an attacker to execute arbitrary code.
        String command = "convert " + userInput + " output.png";
        Runtime.getRuntime().exec(command);
    }
}
```
***
```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.List;

public class CommandInjectionExample {
    // This method is secure because it uses an argument array instead of a single string
    public void processImage(String userInput) throws IOException {
        // Instead of a single string, we pass the command and its arguments as a List of strings.
        // This prevents the OS from interpreting shell metacharacters (like ; or &) within the arguments.
        ProcessBuilder pb = new ProcessBuilder("convert", userInput, "output.png");

        // Redirect error stream to handle potential issues safely
        pb.redirectErrorStream(true);
        pb.start();
    }
}
```

